WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:03.269
-Our first guest tonight is
an Academy Award-nominated

00:00:03.269 --> 00:00:04.703
actress you know
from films such as "Mank,"

00:00:04.703 --> 00:00:06.005
"Mamma Mia!", and "Mean Girls."

00:00:06.005 --> 00:00:08.507
She stars as Elizabeth Holmes
in "The Dropout,"

00:00:08.507 --> 00:00:10.310
which premieres on Hulu
March 3rd.

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:11.944
Let's take a look.

00:00:11.944 --> 00:00:15.348
-"Here at Theranos...

00:00:15.348 --> 00:00:18.851
we are developing
new technology."

00:00:18.851 --> 00:00:20.052
[ Clears throat ]

00:00:20.052 --> 00:00:23.823
[Deeper voice]
"Here at Theranos...

00:00:23.823 --> 00:00:26.125
we are developing
new technology."

00:00:26.125 --> 00:00:27.894
"New technology."

00:00:27.894 --> 00:00:33.466
"This is an inspiring step...

00:00:33.466 --> 00:00:35.001
forward."

00:00:35.001 --> 00:00:38.304
Forward. Forward.

00:00:38.304 --> 00:00:41.307
-Please welcome back to the
show Amanda Seyfried, everybody!

00:00:41.307 --> 00:00:43.075
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:43.075 --> 00:00:49.282
♪♪

00:00:49.282 --> 00:00:55.455
♪♪

00:00:55.455 --> 00:00:57.723
Hello!

00:00:57.723 --> 00:00:59.759
-It's so warm here.

00:00:59.759 --> 00:01:01.894
-It is so lovely to have you.

00:01:01.894 --> 00:01:04.363
You are such a friend of this
show, and you haven't even --

00:01:04.363 --> 00:01:05.598
It hasn't even been that long

00:01:05.598 --> 00:01:07.033
since the last time
you were here,

00:01:07.033 --> 00:01:08.567
and we just want you to stop by
whenever you feel like it.

00:01:08.567 --> 00:01:10.703
-Thanks. It's warm.
The lighting's warm.

00:01:10.703 --> 00:01:12.772
It's not as cold as it could be,

00:01:12.772 --> 00:01:15.741
and you guys are really,
really sweet, so I --

00:01:15.741 --> 00:01:17.576
-That's nice.
I mean, you just met them.

00:01:17.576 --> 00:01:19.245
You just met them.
-I'm just --

00:01:19.245 --> 00:01:21.881
I feel like, by association,
they must be really nice.

00:01:21.881 --> 00:01:23.649
-Yeah. That's very safe.
That is a safe --

00:01:23.649 --> 00:01:27.019
hey, I always do like to check
in on the animals in your life.

00:01:27.019 --> 00:01:28.955
-Yeah.
-You live a farm life.

00:01:28.955 --> 00:01:32.224
You have much in the way
of animal pets.

00:01:32.224 --> 00:01:34.626
-I do.
-And how are they all doing?

00:01:34.626 --> 00:01:40.566
-Yeah, I mean, everybody
was accounted for this morning.

00:01:40.566 --> 00:01:42.802
-So that's a big part
of your morning, is accounting.

00:01:42.802 --> 00:01:45.671
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:01:45.671 --> 00:01:48.774
It's funny. Accounting.
Not the same as math.

00:01:48.774 --> 00:01:50.243
-No.

00:01:50.243 --> 00:01:52.011
-I do have to count
all the chickens

00:01:52.011 --> 00:01:53.279
'cause we have so many.

00:01:53.279 --> 00:01:55.748
I brought you some eggs.
-Did you really?

00:01:55.748 --> 00:01:57.616
-Yeah, they happen
to be right here.

00:01:57.616 --> 00:01:59.685
I actually was late --
Not kidding.

00:01:59.685 --> 00:02:04.123
I was late, so late today
because I forgot the eggs

00:02:04.123 --> 00:02:05.992
and I had said
I would bring them.

00:02:05.992 --> 00:02:08.961
And so I was like, "I have to
keep my word," so I went back.

00:02:08.961 --> 00:02:12.731
It was really out of the way,
but anyway...

00:02:12.731 --> 00:02:15.735
-You're making me feel
a little bad about this gift.

00:02:15.735 --> 00:02:17.169
-I'm sure you have
something for me.

00:02:17.169 --> 00:02:18.571
-You shouldn't give a gift
and go,

00:02:18.571 --> 00:02:20.340
"Anyway,
it was really out of the way."

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:22.074
-I mean, but it was like --

00:02:22.074 --> 00:02:25.111
I just wanted you to know
how much I appreciate my eggs

00:02:25.111 --> 00:02:26.445
and I really love giving eggs.

00:02:26.445 --> 00:02:28.648
-And these are laid
this morning?

00:02:28.648 --> 00:02:30.349
-Those were yesterday.
I did it last night.

00:02:30.349 --> 00:02:32.217
I set it aside.
-Okay, great.

00:02:32.217 --> 00:02:34.286
-And I put the ribbon on.

00:02:34.286 --> 00:02:36.389
I got to Amazon new egg crates.

00:02:36.389 --> 00:02:40.193
But, yeah, no,
it's a great gift.

00:02:40.193 --> 00:02:41.827
I just love them,
and, you know, we have --

00:02:41.827 --> 00:02:43.162
-We love fresh eggs
in our family.

00:02:43.162 --> 00:02:44.831
This is a big deal.
We're very happy about this.

00:02:44.831 --> 00:02:47.033
How many chickens
are you at right now?

00:02:49.335 --> 00:02:51.104
-Uh...
-How many you have.

00:02:51.104 --> 00:02:52.839
-Ten, ten, ten, ten!
-Ten?

00:02:52.839 --> 00:02:54.707
-Ten.
-I mean, if you don't know --

00:02:54.707 --> 00:02:59.545
-Ten. Sorry. Yeah, we have ten.
We have ten, but we recently --

00:02:59.545 --> 00:03:03.783
We had a loss, you know,
of a goat.

00:03:03.783 --> 00:03:05.251
-A goat.
-Not a chicken.

00:03:05.251 --> 00:03:06.686
-You mentioned this
on Instagram.

00:03:06.686 --> 00:03:08.254
-So, like, I keep tabs
of all the animals,

00:03:08.254 --> 00:03:09.722
and it's like -- it's farm life.

00:03:09.722 --> 00:03:11.757
-This is a very sad thing to
talk about on National Pig Day.

00:03:11.757 --> 00:03:14.560
I don't know if you know.
-It's Fasnacht Day, as well.

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:15.961
-What day is it?
-Fasnacht.

00:03:15.961 --> 00:03:17.630
-What's fasnacht?
-So, when I saw

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:19.432
the National Pig Day thing,
I was like, "Oh, that's great.

00:03:19.432 --> 00:03:22.434
We could celebrate that."
But, like, fasnacht.

00:03:22.434 --> 00:03:23.836
Doughnuts?

00:03:23.836 --> 00:03:25.304
-What are you talking about?

00:03:25.304 --> 00:03:26.472
[ Laughter ]

00:03:26.472 --> 00:03:27.740
-I feel really embarrassed

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:29.775
'cause I feel like maybe
this is a long-running joke

00:03:29.775 --> 00:03:31.210
that my mom
has been playing on me

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:32.845
just so she would get me
in this position

00:03:32.845 --> 00:03:35.048
to talk about it on TV
and it's not even real.

00:03:35.048 --> 00:03:38.017
-Wait. It's a holiday
called Fasnacht Day?

00:03:38.017 --> 00:03:39.318
-Yes!
Like, every few months --

00:03:39.318 --> 00:03:41.187
-Wait. Do you really know it?
-Tell me it's real.

00:03:41.187 --> 00:03:43.522
-Okay.
-Whew!

00:03:43.522 --> 00:03:45.758
I was like, "Wow.
My mom is so smart.

00:03:45.758 --> 00:03:46.893
And she plays the long game."

00:03:46.893 --> 00:03:49.662
-Is fasnacht a kind of doughnut?

00:03:49.662 --> 00:03:51.664
-Yes!
-Okay, great.

00:03:51.664 --> 00:03:53.766
We're really leaning
on the audience.

00:03:53.766 --> 00:03:56.002
-Thank you! So, it's real.

00:03:56.002 --> 00:03:58.605
So, happy Pig Day.
Happy Doughnut Day.

00:03:58.605 --> 00:04:01.707
-Yeah, so feed a weird doughnut
to your pig, by all means.

00:04:01.707 --> 00:04:05.277
Alright, so,
which of the goats is lost?

00:04:05.277 --> 00:04:06.846
-[ Audience awws ]
-Aw, that's very sad.

00:04:06.846 --> 00:04:08.848
-She was pretty old, and,
you know, she had a great life.

00:04:08.848 --> 00:04:10.016
-That's really sweet.
-I know.

00:04:10.016 --> 00:04:13.786
It's really weird
when you lose animals

00:04:13.786 --> 00:04:16.322
that didn't live in your house.

00:04:16.322 --> 00:04:18.958
-Well, sorry. Again --
-I don't know. I actually --

00:04:18.958 --> 00:04:21.260
-I mean, I think it'd be weird
if they lived

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:22.728
in your house, too.

00:04:22.728 --> 00:04:24.596
-Well, no, I mean,
like, dogs and cats.

00:04:24.596 --> 00:04:25.931
-Oh, I see what you mean.

00:04:25.931 --> 00:04:28.700
Yes, of course. Yeah.
To lose a farm animal, right.

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:30.603
'Cause I would imagine
it's a little -- Is it easier?

00:04:30.603 --> 00:04:32.771
But you seem so connected
to your farm animals.

00:04:32.771 --> 00:04:33.939
-It is easy, in a way.

00:04:33.939 --> 00:04:35.608
Like, I guess
I'm just used to it.

00:04:35.608 --> 00:04:37.242
Isn't that weird,
to be used to that?

00:04:37.242 --> 00:04:39.612
-Yeah, but I think
that's part of the --

00:04:39.612 --> 00:04:42.848
I don't know -- the wonder
of farm life, is you do learn --

00:04:42.848 --> 00:04:44.684
Carter -- the circle of life.

00:04:44.684 --> 00:04:47.086
[ Laughter ]

00:04:47.086 --> 00:04:49.222
-Yeah. Wow.

00:04:49.222 --> 00:04:50.757
-I'm sorry.

00:04:50.757 --> 00:04:54.593
I should've told you to put on
your noise-canceling as I...

00:04:54.593 --> 00:04:56.228
You know, I don't know --
We've talked a lot

00:04:56.228 --> 00:04:58.431
about the animals
that live on your farm.

00:04:58.431 --> 00:05:01.033
Do you know the goat story
of my life?

00:05:01.033 --> 00:05:03.736
This -- There was a real story
in my life.

00:05:03.736 --> 00:05:05.971
My wife's father --
my father-in-law --

00:05:05.971 --> 00:05:07.640
before he was my father-in-law

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:09.075
and I was just dating
his daughter --

00:05:09.075 --> 00:05:11.344
he had a goat named Raisin,
who is also no longer with us.

00:05:11.344 --> 00:05:13.712
-That is like
the Hercules of goats!

00:05:13.712 --> 00:05:16.615
-It's a beautiful goat.
-That is a stunner.

00:05:16.615 --> 00:05:19.352
-This is proof that
Raisin and I were close.

00:05:19.352 --> 00:05:21.287
And Raisin was
one of the reasons --

00:05:21.287 --> 00:05:23.289
-Oh, my God.
His head against yours.

00:05:23.289 --> 00:05:24.923
-I know. It's really sweet.

00:05:24.923 --> 00:05:26.959
And that's why I wanted to --
-Raisin's no longer...

00:05:26.959 --> 00:05:28.828
-He's no longer with us, yeah.

00:05:28.828 --> 00:05:30.363
He moved to L.A. to be an actor.

00:05:30.363 --> 00:05:31.997
-Okay.
-[ Laughter ]

00:05:31.997 --> 00:05:34.567
-Do you find goats to be --
My father-in-law

00:05:34.567 --> 00:05:36.402
swears by them
as very affectionate animals.

00:05:36.402 --> 00:05:37.870
Are your goats affectionate?

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:41.741
-Yeah, we have some dudes
that just --

00:05:41.741 --> 00:05:44.710
Too cool for what. I don't know.
But too cool for me, for sure.

00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:49.482
But then we have one
who's cool, really cute.

00:05:49.482 --> 00:05:51.283
And then we have two
who are just --

00:05:51.283 --> 00:05:53.519
Oh, God, they're just amazing.

00:05:53.519 --> 00:05:57.523
They were just like Josie,
actually, who we lost.

00:05:57.523 --> 00:06:00.659
But they're amazing, and
they love getting their horns --

00:06:00.659 --> 00:06:02.361
-Yeah, that's a great scratch.

00:06:02.361 --> 00:06:03.696
Scratching a goat
is a good feeling.

00:06:03.696 --> 00:06:05.164
-Yeah, and the smell
on your hands.

00:06:05.164 --> 00:06:08.334
It's really actually
a very cathartic experience.

00:06:08.334 --> 00:06:10.469
-Yeah.
-For both of you.

00:06:10.469 --> 00:06:11.737
-Yeah.
-It's crazy.

00:06:11.737 --> 00:06:14.006
-You should open up,
like, a New York spa

00:06:14.006 --> 00:06:16.575
where people
just come in and rub a goat's...

00:06:16.575 --> 00:06:19.479
-You know what? That's
the thing about the goat yoga.

00:06:19.479 --> 00:06:21.547
Like, I'm not tapping
into a new thing.

00:06:21.547 --> 00:06:22.882
-You're not doing it. Yeah.

00:06:22.882 --> 00:06:24.884
-It's like, no,
they've already been tapped.

00:06:24.884 --> 00:06:27.253
-Yes. Have you done goat yoga?

00:06:27.253 --> 00:06:28.621
-No.
-Yeah.

00:06:28.621 --> 00:06:29.988
-Why would you --
-Yeah, I don't --

00:06:29.988 --> 00:06:31.957
-Why would you --
I don't know. Maybe it's great.

00:06:31.957 --> 00:06:33.492
Actually, I'm not gonna
knock it, but, like, they --

00:06:33.492 --> 00:06:34.793
I don't know if you know this,

00:06:34.793 --> 00:06:38.964
but they don't have any control
of when they poop.

00:06:38.964 --> 00:06:41.033
So why would you want to --
-Yeah, exactly.

00:06:41.033 --> 00:06:43.669
So, you're playing
Elizabeth Holmes, and, you know,

00:06:43.669 --> 00:06:46.672
I think this is a story
that even those of us

00:06:46.672 --> 00:06:51.110
who don't follow, you know,
business or that world,

00:06:51.110 --> 00:06:52.478
we all learned
about Elizabeth Holmes

00:06:52.478 --> 00:06:54.113
and Theranos and this company.

00:06:54.113 --> 00:06:56.749
Did you know much about her
when this came to you?

00:06:56.749 --> 00:06:58.717
-I did, 'cause I listened
to "The Dropout."

00:06:58.717 --> 00:07:00.386
-Which is a fantastic podcast,
yes.

00:07:00.386 --> 00:07:02.788
-Such a good podcast.
I was into podcasts.

00:07:02.788 --> 00:07:05.024
And I was sitting around
and I listened to it,

00:07:05.024 --> 00:07:06.959
and I was like,
"Wow. This is fascinating."

00:07:06.959 --> 00:07:10.128
And then, you know, I just,
like, went on my judgy way.

00:07:10.128 --> 00:07:12.064
-Yeah.
-And then this comes up

00:07:12.064 --> 00:07:13.766
a couple --
like a year and a half later,

00:07:13.766 --> 00:07:16.502
and I'm like,
"I'm gonna really --

00:07:16.502 --> 00:07:18.471
I got to come at it
from a different angle."

00:07:18.471 --> 00:07:21.206
And, sorry, but we're --
If you think about it,

00:07:21.206 --> 00:07:23.342
I mean, as actors,
like, we're all human.

00:07:23.342 --> 00:07:26.178
-Yes.
-We know that, and our ability

00:07:26.178 --> 00:07:30.983
to be compassionate
is hopefully working.

00:07:30.983 --> 00:07:35.922
And, gosh, what a complicated
person to be able to play.

00:07:35.922 --> 00:07:37.422
I feel so lucky.

00:07:37.422 --> 00:07:39.792
And yet I don't know
anything about her,

00:07:39.792 --> 00:07:46.331
but her story is as fascinating
or more than it was before.

00:07:46.331 --> 00:07:48.334
-While you were filming,
she was found guilty

00:07:48.334 --> 00:07:49.602
on multiple counts.
-Yes, crazy.

00:07:49.602 --> 00:07:51.470
-That is obviously
a crazy thing to happen

00:07:51.470 --> 00:07:52.972
in the middle of while you're --

00:07:52.972 --> 00:07:55.274
-We had dumped
like 700 text messages

00:07:55.274 --> 00:07:57.576
one night
in the middle of filming.

00:07:57.576 --> 00:07:59.478
And Liz Meriwether,
the genius that she is,

00:07:59.478 --> 00:08:01.613
she's like,
"We're gonna use these."

00:08:01.613 --> 00:08:03.616
And, in fact,
one of the text messages

00:08:03.616 --> 00:08:05.851
was referring to a baby bird.

00:08:05.851 --> 00:08:08.687
And I was like, "Oh, my God.
There was a baby bird."

00:08:08.687 --> 00:08:10.589
And she's like, "No, I thought
she was referring to him

00:08:10.589 --> 00:08:11.824
as 'Baby Bird.'"

00:08:11.824 --> 00:08:13.325
And it's like,
"What is this baby bird?

00:08:13.325 --> 00:08:15.894
Do we need a baby bird
on the show?

00:08:15.894 --> 00:08:18.463
How do we incorporate a bird?

00:08:18.463 --> 00:08:21.133
And, like, what scene
is it gonna be in?

00:08:21.133 --> 00:08:24.169
And -- Or is it a metaphor
for something, you know?"

00:08:24.169 --> 00:08:27.773
And it was a real,
like, mind eff.

00:08:27.773 --> 00:08:30.176
-Are you not gonna tell us
what the bird was?

00:08:30.176 --> 00:08:33.279
-I don't know!
-We still don't know?

00:08:33.279 --> 00:08:35.715
-No, but, also, apparently
we couldn't afford a baby bird.

00:08:35.715 --> 00:08:37.249
-Really?
-And the line producer

00:08:37.249 --> 00:08:38.350
was like, "We can't."
-Really?

00:08:38.350 --> 00:08:39.751
-And we're like, "Alright."
I guess --

00:08:39.751 --> 00:08:41.387
Because we don't know anyway,
but we were just guessing.

00:08:41.387 --> 00:08:42.521
It's all guesses.

00:08:42.521 --> 00:08:43.822
-By the way,
the show looks fantastic.

00:08:43.822 --> 00:08:45.558
Like, it seems like it has
a pretty robust budget.

00:08:45.558 --> 00:08:49.127
I can't believe baby bird
was, like, the kill point.

00:08:49.127 --> 00:08:50.929
-Oh.
-"Oh, we could do a baby bird,

00:08:50.929 --> 00:08:52.665
but there's no pizza
at the crew party."

00:08:52.665 --> 00:08:54.534
-Nobody gets fed for a week.

00:08:54.534 --> 00:08:56.234
-Oh, yeah,
you got to feed the birds.

00:08:56.234 --> 00:08:57.803
You know.
You got that farm life.

00:08:57.803 --> 00:08:59.171
You're like,
"It's never just a goat!"

00:08:59.171 --> 00:09:00.506
-It's nev-- You're right.

00:09:00.506 --> 00:09:01.974
You don't know
what baby birds eat.

00:09:01.974 --> 00:09:03.275
You don't know what they need.

00:09:03.275 --> 00:09:06.145
-They're growing birds.
-Could be super expensive.

00:09:06.145 --> 00:09:08.447
-You -- One of the things --

00:09:08.447 --> 00:09:09.948
You've played
real people before.

00:09:09.948 --> 00:09:11.817
You've never played
a living person before.

00:09:11.817 --> 00:09:13.285
-True.

00:09:13.285 --> 00:09:16.489
-And a person who's, you know,
in the same generation as you.

00:09:16.489 --> 00:09:20.493
And I did read that
when you were in the bedroom set

00:09:20.493 --> 00:09:22.160
that you had built,
you realized

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:24.497
that this is a person you also
had things in common with.

00:09:24.497 --> 00:09:25.865
-We're essentially the same age.

00:09:25.865 --> 00:09:27.066
She had, like,
the "Seventeen" --

00:09:27.066 --> 00:09:30.469
I remember reading that issue.
It wasn't "she."

00:09:30.469 --> 00:09:33.005
I have to remember.
This is a set.

00:09:33.005 --> 00:09:35.040
It was designed
by a production designer.

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:36.441
We had a set dresser.

00:09:36.441 --> 00:09:39.245
But I remember, like,
it really connected me to her,

00:09:39.245 --> 00:09:41.781
'cause we're contemporaries.

00:09:41.781 --> 00:09:44.683
We were brought up in the same
world where the same movies

00:09:44.683 --> 00:09:46.585
were playing and, like,
we knew the same music.

00:09:46.585 --> 00:09:49.521
I don't know her favorite.
We think we do.

00:09:49.521 --> 00:09:52.091
But it was just --
it really tied me to her.

00:09:52.091 --> 00:09:55.127
And it was also amazing to see
these, like, "YM" magazines

00:09:55.127 --> 00:09:57.296
and that magazine
that had the stickers in it.

00:09:57.296 --> 00:09:59.131
You don't know,
'cause you were a boy.

00:09:59.131 --> 00:10:02.401
But, like, remember when the
magazines came with stickers?

00:10:02.401 --> 00:10:04.737
-What were the stickers of?
-Boy bands.

00:10:04.737 --> 00:10:07.306
-Oh, yeah.
-Chad Michael Murray.

00:10:07.306 --> 00:10:09.174
-[ Cheers and applause ]
-I don't know.

00:10:09.174 --> 00:10:11.544
-I'm very confused
by this group of people tonight.

00:10:11.544 --> 00:10:13.144
[ Laughter ]

00:10:13.144 --> 00:10:15.948
Like, I get if you had named
your favorite boy band,

00:10:15.948 --> 00:10:18.851
but they just went for, like,
that description of a band.

00:10:18.851 --> 00:10:21.653
-Well, because boy bands
are a thing of the past-ish.

00:10:21.653 --> 00:10:22.822
-Ish. I think it's back.

00:10:22.822 --> 00:10:24.223
-There were a lot more
than there are now.

00:10:24.223 --> 00:10:26.192
-Yeah, they all died off,
like the dinosaurs.

00:10:26.192 --> 00:10:27.393
-Well, they didn't, though,

00:10:27.393 --> 00:10:28.894
because they had
a Christmas special.

00:10:28.894 --> 00:10:31.797
Whatever. I didn't see it.
I missed it, but I --

00:10:31.797 --> 00:10:36.702
-It is a really --
It is a really cool role,

00:10:36.702 --> 00:10:38.770
playing
a very complicated person.

00:10:38.770 --> 00:10:40.272
And you're very, very good
at this.

00:10:40.272 --> 00:10:42.274
And thank you so much
for being back on the show.

00:10:42.274 --> 00:10:44.010
It's always a delight.
-It's so exciting.

00:10:44.010 --> 00:10:47.245
Thanks for having me, always.
-Amanda Seyfried, everybody.

00:10:47.245 --> 00:10:49.882
"The Dropout" premieres on Hulu
this Thursday,

00:10:49.882 --> 00:10:52.118
with new episodes airing weekly
through April 7th.

